leads to an overall depolarization for the larger clusters, decreasing the error significantly in comparison to TDLDA. The depolarization is even larger for the Si bulk, in which the TS-vdW scheme yields an overestimation of \(68 \%\) in the \(C_{6}^{\mathrm{Si}-\mathrm{Si}}\) coefficient in comparison to the value derived from the experimental dielectric function, whereas the TS-vdW + SCS method is in excellent agreement with experiment, with an error of just \(8 \%\).

We now extend the TS-vdW + SCS method to include the fully nonadditive many-body vdW energy. For a system with \(N\) atoms, the many-body vdW energy will contain terms up to \(N\) th order (i.e., 2 -body, 3 -body, \(\ldots, N\)-body). First, the fully screened static polarizability \(\alpha_{p}^{\text {SCS }}\) and the characteristic excitation frequency \(\omega_{p}^{\text {SCS }}\) are obtained for every atom in the system using the SCS procedure described above [20]. The many-body vdW energy is computed using the coupled fluctuating dipole model (CFDM) [6,21] for a collection of coupled isotropic threedimensional QHOs that represent the atoms in the molecular system of interest. The CFDM Hamiltonian can be written as [21]
\[
\begin{aligned}
H= & -\frac{1}{2} \sum_{p=1}^{N} \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\chi}_{p}}^{2}+\frac{1}{2} \sum_{p=1}^{N} \omega_{p}^{2} \chi_{p}^{2} \\
& +\sum_{p>q}^{N} \omega_{p} \omega_{q} \sqrt{\alpha_{p} \alpha_{q}} \boldsymbol{\chi}_{p} \mathcal{T}_{p q}^{\prime} \boldsymbol{\chi}_{q}
\end{aligned}
\]
where \(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{p}=\sqrt{m_{p}} \boldsymbol{\mu}_{p}\), with \(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{p}\) being the displacement of oscillator \(p\) from equilibrium, and \(m_{p}=\left(\alpha_{p} \omega_{p}^{2}\right)^{-1}\). In the above equation, the first two terms correspond to the kinetic and potential energy for an individual QHO, while the third term corresponds to the dipole-dipole interaction between QHOs \(\left[\mathcal{T}_{p q}^{\prime}=\nabla_{\mathbf{r}_{p}} \otimes \nabla_{\mathbf{r}_{q}} W^{\prime}\left(r_{p q}\right)\right.\), where \(W^{\prime}\left(r_{p q}\right)\) will be defined below]. The SCS superscript is ommited in the notation, but assumed for all \(\alpha\) and \(\omega\). The bilinear form of the interaction term in the CFDM Hamiltonian
allows for a numerically exact solution of the Schrödinger equation by diagonalizing the \(3 N \times 3 N\) interaction matrix. The vdW interaction energy between the QHOs is then computed as the difference between the zero-point energies of the coupled and uncoupled QHOs
\[
E_{\mathrm{vdW}}=\frac{1}{2} \sum_{p=1}^{3 N} \sqrt{\lambda_{p}}-\frac{3}{2} \sum_{p=1}^{N} \omega_{p}^{\mathrm{SCS}},
\]
where \(\lambda_{p}\) are the Hamiltonian eigenvalues. The above expression for the vdW energy can be derived from ACFDT, since in our case the polarizability is an even function of \(\omega\) and has only simple poles on or below the real \(\omega\) axis. Under these circumstances the ACFDT correlation energy is given as the difference of zero-point energies [22,23].

The many-body vdW energy is part of the long-range correlation energy, but in general the correlation energy includes other contributions. In order to have an electronic structure method that treats the full range of exchange and correlation effects, we need to couple the vdW energy to an approximate DFT functional. This coupling typically introduces empiricism into the approach. However, instead of the ad hoc damping functions used in interatomic pairwise dispersion-correction approaches, here the coupling is more naturally introduced through a modified Coulomb potential [24]. We take an arbitrary, albeit one of the simplest, forms for the Coulomb potential
\[
W^{\prime}\left(r_{p q}\right)=\left\{1-\exp \left[-\left(r_{p q} / R_{p q}^{\mathrm{vdW}}\right)^{\beta}\right]\right\} / r_{p q},
\]
where \(\beta\) is a range-separation parameter and \(R_{p q}^{v d W}\) is the sum of the vdW radii for a pair of atoms \(p\) and \(q\). The parameter \(\beta\) controls how quickly the Coulomb potential reaches the \(1 / r_{p q}\) asymptote in the long range. Physically, the dispersion energy is finite in the zero-distance limit [25]; however, approximate DFT functionals should already capture part of this energy. Thus we choose \(\beta \geq 2\), which ensures that \(W^{\prime}\left(r_{p q}\right) \rightarrow 0\) when \(r_{p q} \rightarrow 0\). In Eq. (7) the vdW radius of an atom is defined as in the TS-vdW scheme, \(R_{p}^{\text {vdW }}=\left(\alpha_{p}^{\text {SCS }} / \alpha_{p}^{\text {free }}\right)^{1 / 3} R_{p}^{\text {vdW, free }}\), where the free atom vdW radius is obtained from the coupled-cluster electron density. This accurate reference electron density is required for a consistent definition of the vdW radius with the polarizability [5].

The combination of the TS-vdW scheme with SCS and CFDM gives rise to an efficient method for obtaining the vdW energy, wherein short- and long-range screening effects are seamlessly included and the many-body vdW energy is computed to infinite order in the dipole approximation. In what follows we call this new scheme DFA + MBD, where DFA is substituted by the name of the functional used to approximate the DFT energy, and MBD means many-body dispersion. We benchmarked the performance of DFA + MBD on the S22 database of intermolecular interactions [26,27]. All DFA calculations employ the FHI-AIMS code [28]. The \(\beta\) parameter in

